# What stays out of the build context sent to `docker build`.
# Smaller context means faster builds, and a few of these would actively break
# the image if copied in.

# `content` is a symlink to a path on the host machine. Copying it into the image produces a
# dangling symlink; the real content arrives at runtime as a volume mount.
content

# Not needed to build the site, and .git in particular is large. Note this is
# the *repo's* history — page dates come from the vault's git history, which
# lives in the mounted volume, not here.
.git
.github
README.md

# Rebuilt inside the image, and host-specific besides. node_modules would also
# carry macOS-native binaries (sharp, @napi-rs/simple-git) into a Linux image.
node_modules
public
prof
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Plugin cache and index — regenerated by `npm run install-plugins` during the
# build, so a stale copy from the host would only confuse things.
.quartz
.quartz-cache

# Deployment files: read by docker/compose on the host, never needed inside.
# (docker/entrypoint.sh is copied explicitly by the Dockerfile, so the docker/
# directory itself stays in the context.)
compose.yaml
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
.env
.env.example

# Noise
.DS_Store
*.log
